course: dictionary
lesson: 8359
title: A tiny animal with spinning wheels
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A rotifer is a tiny water animal. You usually cannot see it with your eyes alone, so you need a microscope. «Rotifers are tiny animals with a crown of beating hairs that looks like spinning wheels.» The clues are tiny, animals, and beating hairs.
explain: Look at the hairs. They beat and wave at the animal's head, so they look like spinning wheels. The name comes from Latin and means "wheel-bearer," which fits what you can see. Use the clues to work out that a rotifer is an animal.
explain: Scientists find rotifers in pond water when they look through a microscope. Even though both are tiny, a rotifer is an animal, not a bacterium. Always check the clues in the sentence before you decide what a word means.
fact: A rotifer is a tiny water animal, usually too small to see without a microscope.
fact: The ring of little hairs at a rotifer's head beats like spinning wheels. This is where its name comes from.
fact: "Rotifer" comes from Latin and means "wheel-bearer."
fact: Rotifers are animals, not bacteria, even though both can be tiny.
fact: Some rotifers can dry out for years and come back to life when they get wet.
exercise: What is a rotifer? | *A tiny water animal, usually too small to see without a microscope | A plant with spinning leaves | A type of bacteria that makes you ill | A large fish that lives in the sea
exercise: Why does the name rotifer mean "wheel-bearer"? | It spins round and round in the sea | *Its head has beating hairs that look like spinning wheels | It was named after a bicycle | It rolls along on tiny wheels
exercise: Where would you most likely find rotifers? | In a bag of sugar | On the top of a mountain | *In pond water, under a microscope | In a desert sand dune
test: Are rotifers bacteria? | Yes, they are tiny bacteria | *No, they are tiny animals | Yes, they are a kind of plant | No, they are tiny rocks
test: «Some were reported to revive after about 24,000 years frozen.» What does revive mean here? | *To come back to life | To break into pieces | To sink to the bottom of a pond | To freeze solid and stop
exam: What does a rotifer's crown of beating hairs look like? | *Spinning wheels | A crown of flowers | Tiny fish fins | A ball of wool
